Read it — it's a 7-line file. Here's the finding.

## The problem

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;  // repeated reallocation
  return out;
}
```

**Cause — avoidable reallocations from repeated appends.** `out` starts empty and grows via `+=`. Each append that exceeds the current capacity forces the `std::string` to allocate a larger buffer and **copy the entire accumulated content** into it. With geometric growth that's O(n log n) total copy work plus heap churn, and it's entirely avoidable here because the final size is known up front.

## The idiomatic fix — `reserve` when the total size is known

Compute the sum of all part sizes first, reserve once, then append:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::size_t total = 0;
  for (const auto& p : parts) total += p.size();
  out.reserve(total);          // single allocation, no regrowth
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

This turns the loop into a single allocation + one linear copy pass — O(n) with no reallocations.

